IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- THE CORE: the shared vocabulary of the Money Laundering, Terrorist Financing and
-- Transfer of Funds (Information on the Payer) Regulations 2017 (S.I. 2017/692), United
-- Kingdom.  Nouns only: DECLARE and shared constants, no rules.  The text is a plain-text
-- rendering made by a converter script; it is not the official text, and nothing here is
-- verified against the official text.
--
-- THE SEAT.  A business that is a relevant person (a bank, an accountancy or legal firm, an
-- estate agent, a money service business ...) and its compliance officer, asking, on facts
-- they can state, whether the Regulations apply, what customer due diligence is needed for a
-- customer or a transaction, what to record and keep, and by when.
--
-- CONVENTIONS EVERY PART FOLLOWS
--  * Money is a NUMBER of pounds sterling (reg 3(2)(a): an amount in another currency is
--    compared through its sterling equivalent, reg 3(2)(b)).  Dates are DATE, written
--    `Date day month year`.  A period is a NUMBER of days unless a comment says otherwise.
--  * A constructor is unique across the core, so a part never redeclares one.
--  * A field is named from the complement, never `is ...` or `has ...`.
--  * The core holds only what a witness of ordinary competence could testify to ("we are an
--    estate agency", "the customer is a trust", "the payment was in cash").  Whether that
--    makes the business a relevant person, or the trust's trustee a beneficial owner, is a
--    rule and lives with the regulation that says so.

§ `Who is who`

-- Reg 8(2) lists the persons to whom the Regulations apply, and reg 8(3) adds the auction
-- platform for the provisions it names.  A business can be several kinds at once (a law firm
-- can be an independent legal professional and a trust or company service provider), so a
-- case carries a LIST OF this type.  Which kind a business IS is decided by regs 10 to 14A
-- from the facts; the core only names the kinds.
-- src:239 | (2) The persons listed in this paragraph are—
-- src:240 | (a) credit institutions;
-- src:241 | (b) financial institutions;
-- src:242 | (c) auditors, insolvency practitioners, external accountants and tax advisers;
-- src:243 | (d) independent legal professionals;
-- src:244 | (e) trust or company service providers;
-- src:245 | (f) estate agents and letting agents;
-- src:246 | (g) high value dealers;
-- src:247 | (h) casinos;
-- src:248 | (i) art market participants;
-- src:249 | (j) cryptoasset exchange providers;
-- src:250 | (k) custodian wallet providers.
-- src:251 | (3) Regulations 3, 7, 9, 15, 17 to 21, 24, 25, 46, 47, 50 to 52, 65 to 82, 84, 86 to 93, 101, 102 and 106 apply to an auction platform acting in the course of business carried on by it in the United Kingdom, and such an auction platform is a relevant person for the purposes of those provisions.
@ref reg 8(2) and (3) (src:239-251)
DECLARE `Kind of relevant person` IS ONE OF
    `credit institution`
    `financial institution`
    `auditor`
    `insolvency practitioner`
    `external accountant`
    `tax adviser`
    `independent legal professional`
    `trust or company service provider`
    `estate agent`
    `letting agent`
    `high value dealer`
    `casino`
    `art market participant`
    `cryptoasset exchange provider`
    `custodian wallet provider`
    `auction platform`

